WHY DO WE USE A VISITED SET?

Without tracking visited vertices, we would go in circles forever!

Example of what would happen without visited set:
  0 goes to 1
  1 goes back to 0
  0 goes back to 1
  1 goes back to 0
  ... infinite loop!

The visited set prevents us from revisiting nodes we have already explored.


RECURSION IN DFS

Recursion means a function calling itself.

DFS naturally uses recursion because:
  - When you visit a neighbor, you do the SAME thing
    (visit its neighbors, then their neighbors, and so on)
  - The recursion automatically handles backtracking
    (when the function returns, execution goes back to where it was called)

The Call Stack for DFS looks like this:

dfs(0)
  calls dfs(1)
    calls dfs(3)
      returns (dead end, no unvisited neighbors)
    calls dfs(4)
      returns (dead end)
    returns (all neighbors explored)
  calls dfs(2)
    calls dfs(5)
      returns (dead end)
    calls dfs(6)
      returns (dead end)
    returns (all neighbors explored)
  returns (all done)


TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY

Time Complexity:  O(V + E)
  - V = number of vertices
  - E = number of edges
  - We visit each vertex exactly once (O(V))
  - We check each edge exactly once (O(E))
  - Total: O(V + E)

Space Complexity: O(V)
  - The visited set stores at most V vertices
  - The recursion call stack goes at most V levels deep
  - Total extra space: O(V)


APPLICATIONS OF DFS

  1. Maze solving          - Finding a path through a maze
  2. Topological sorting   - Ordering tasks with dependencies
  3. Cycle detection       - Finding loops in a graph
  4. Connected components  - Finding all connected parts
  5. Solving puzzles       - Sudoku, N-Queens, etc.
  6. Web crawling          - Following links from page to page


