------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY
------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHAT IS A GREEDY ALGORITHM?

A Greedy Algorithm makes the LOCALLY OPTIMAL choice at each step,
hoping that these local choices lead to a globally optimal solution.

Key characteristics of Greedy Algorithms:
  - At each step, pick the best option currently available
  - Never look back or reconsider past choices
  - Simple to understand and implement
  - Fast execution
  - Does NOT always give the globally optimal solution for all problems
    (works perfectly for some problems, fails for others)

Real-life analogy:
  Imagine you are collecting coins scattered on a path.
  Greedy approach: Always pick up the largest coin you can see right now.
  This might not give you the maximum total if a smaller coin leads to
  a pile of large coins around the corner.

Another analogy:
  Imagine hiking in fog to the highest peak.
  Greedy approach: Always step in the direction that goes most uphill.
  This might get you to a local peak, not the highest mountain.


WHAT IS SELECTION SORT?

Selection Sort is a simple comparison-based sorting algorithm.

The main idea:
  1. The array is divided into two parts:
     - Left part: Already sorted
     - Right part: Not yet sorted
  2. Initially, the sorted part is empty and unsorted part is the whole array
  3. Repeatedly find the MINIMUM element in the unsorted part
  4. SWAP it to the end of the sorted part (beginning of unsorted part)
  5. The sorted part grows by one element each iteration

WHY IS SELECTION SORT A GREEDY ALGORITHM?

At each step, Selection Sort greedily picks the MINIMUM (the locally best choice)
from the remaining unsorted elements and places it in its correct position.

This greedy choice (always pick the minimum next) is applied repeatedly
until the entire array is sorted.

The greedy strategy works perfectly here because:
  - The smallest available element definitely belongs at the current position
  - Placing it there never makes the overall sorting worse
  - Each greedy choice is globally optimal in this context


SELECTION SORT STEP-BY-STEP EXAMPLE

Original Array: [64, 25, 12, 22, 11]

Positions:       0   1   2   3   4

Step 1 (i=0):
  Find minimum in entire array [64, 25, 12, 22, 11]
  Minimum is 11 at index 4
  Swap arr[0]=64 with arr[4]=11
  Result: [11, 25, 12, 22, 64]
  Sorted: [11]  |  Unsorted: [25, 12, 22, 64]

Step 2 (i=1):
  Find minimum in unsorted part [25, 12, 22, 64]
  Minimum is 12 at index 2
  Swap arr[1]=25 with arr[2]=12
  Result: [11, 12, 25, 22, 64]
  Sorted: [11, 12]  |  Unsorted: [25, 22, 64]

Step 3 (i=2):
  Find minimum in unsorted part [25, 22, 64]
  Minimum is 22 at index 3
  Swap arr[2]=25 with arr[3]=22
  Result: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]
  Sorted: [11, 12, 22]  |  Unsorted: [25, 64]

Step 4 (i=3):
  Find minimum in unsorted part [25, 64]
  Minimum is 25 at index 3
  Swap arr[3]=25 with arr[3]=25 (same index, no actual change)
  Result: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]
  Sorted: [11, 12, 22, 25]  |  Unsorted: [64]

Step 5 (i=4):
  Only one element left [64], it must already be in place
  Result: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]
  Sorted: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]

Final Sorted Array: [11, 12, 22, 25, 64]


TIME COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

The outer loop runs n times (for each position i from 0 to n-1).
The inner loop runs n-1, n-2, n-3, ..., 1 times.

Total comparisons = (n-1) + (n-2) + (n-3) + ... + 1
                  = n * (n-1) / 2
                  = O(n^2)

Best case:  O(n^2)  Even if array is already sorted, we still do all comparisons
Average:    O(n^2)
Worst case: O(n^2)

Space complexity: O(1)
  Sorts the array IN-PLACE (modifies the original array)
  Uses only a few extra variables (min_index, loop counters)
  No extra arrays needed


ADVANTAGE OF SELECTION SORT

Selection Sort makes at most n-1 swaps (one per outer loop iteration).
This is useful in scenarios where SWAP operations are expensive.

For comparison, Bubble Sort can make O(n^2) swaps in the worst case.


