WHAT IS VIRTUALIZATION?

Virtualization is the technology of creating virtual (not physical) versions
of computing resources including:
  - Virtual computers (Virtual Machines)
  - Virtual storage (virtual disks)
  - Virtual networks

Without virtualization: One physical server runs one operating system.
With virtualization: One physical server runs MULTIPLE operating systems
simultaneously, each isolated from the others.

Benefits:
  - Better hardware utilization (run multiple workloads on one machine)
  - Isolation (problem in one VM doesn't affect others)
  - Flexibility (quickly create, clone, snapshot, delete VMs)
  - Cost savings (fewer physical servers needed)


WHAT IS A HYPERVISOR?

A hypervisor (also called Virtual Machine Monitor) is software that:
  - Creates and manages virtual machines
  - Shares physical hardware resources among VMs
  - Provides isolation between VMs

Type 1 Hypervisor (Bare-metal):
  Runs directly on the physical hardware.
  No host OS required.
  More efficient and direct hardware access.
  Examples: VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM

Type 2 Hypervisor (Hosted):
  Runs as an application on a host operating system.
  Host OS runs first, then hypervisor runs on top.
  Easier to install and use.
  Slightly less efficient due to extra layer.
  Examples: VirtualBox, VMware Workstation


WHAT IS KVM?

KVM (Kernel-based Virtual Machine) is:
  - A virtualization solution built into the Linux kernel since 2007
  - Type 1 hypervisor (bare-metal level performance)
  - Open-source and free
  - The hypervisor used under the hood by AWS, Google Cloud, and others

KVM turns the Linux kernel into a hypervisor.
When KVM is installed, Linux itself manages VMs with near-native performance.

Requirements:
  CPU must support hardware virtualization:
    Intel CPU: VT-x (vmx) feature must be enabled in BIOS
    AMD CPU:   AMD-V (svm) feature must be enabled in BIOS
  Check: egrep -c '(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo
    Non-zero output means virtualization is supported


KEY COMPONENTS

QEMU (Quick EMUlator):
  Emulates hardware for virtual machines.
  Provides: Virtual CPU, Virtual RAM, Virtual disk, Virtual network card.
  Works alongside KVM: KVM handles virtualization, QEMU provides device emulation.

libvirt:
  A management layer and API for virtualization platforms.
  Provides:
    virsh: Command-line tool for managing VMs
    virt-manager: Graphical UI for managing VMs
    Python/C API for programmatic VM management
  Makes it easier to manage KVM (and other hypervisors) without low-level commands.

bridge-utils:
  Creates network bridges that allow VMs to connect to the physical network.
  A network bridge is like a virtual network switch.
  With a bridge, VMs can get their own IP addresses on the local network.
  Without a bridge, VMs use NAT (Network Address Translation) which is more isolated.

virt-install:
  Command-line tool to create new VMs.
  Automates the process of creating and configuring VMs.


KEY virsh COMMANDS EXPLAINED

  virsh list --all
    List all virtual machines (both running and stopped).
    Without --all, only running VMs are shown.

  virsh start testvm
    Start the VM named "testvm". Like pressing power button.

  virsh shutdown testvm
    Gracefully shut down "testvm". Sends ACPI shutdown signal.
    The guest OS shuts down cleanly (saving files, etc.).

  virsh destroy testvm
    Forcefully stop "testvm". Like pulling the power cord.
    NOT recommended in production (data loss possible).
    Use virsh shutdown for graceful shutdown.

  virsh snapshot-create-as testvm snap1
    Create a snapshot of "testvm" named "snap1".
    Captures the complete VM state at this point in time.
    You can restore to this state later if something goes wrong.
    Extremely useful before making major system changes.


VIRT-INSTALL COMMAND PARAMETERS EXPLAINED

  sudo virt-install \
    --name testvm         Name of the new VM
    --vcpus 2             Allocate 2 virtual CPU cores
    --memory 2048         Allocate 2048 MB (2 GB) of RAM
    --disk size=20        Create a 20 GB virtual disk
    --cdrom /path/to/iso  Install from this ISO image (like inserting a DVD)
    --os-variant ubuntu22.04  Optimize for Ubuntu 22.04
    --network network=default Use the default (NAT) network
    --graphics none       No graphical display (text-only install)
    --console pty,target_type=serial  Connect via serial console


