APEX SYNTAX COMPARED TO PYTHON

Python:
  def selection_sort(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    for i in range(n):
      min_idx = i
      for j in range(i+1, n):
        if arr[j] < arr[min_idx]:
          min_idx = j
      arr[i], arr[min_idx] = arr[min_idx], arr[i]
    return arr

Apex (similar to Java):
  public static List<Integer> selectionSort(List<Integer> arr) {
    Integer n = arr.size();
    for (Integer i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
      Integer minIdx = i;
      for (Integer j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) {
          minIdx = j;
        }
      }
      if (minIdx != i) {
        Integer temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[minIdx];
        arr[minIdx] = temp;
      }
    }
    return arr;
  }

Key differences:
  1. Type declarations required:
     Python:  min_idx = i         (dynamic typing, no declaration needed)
     Apex:    Integer minIdx = i;  (static typing, must declare type)

  2. Method signatures:
     Python:  def function_name(params):
     Apex:    access_modifier return_type method_name(param_type param_name)

  3. Loops:
     Python:  for i in range(n):
     Apex:    for (Integer i = 0; i < n; i++)

  4. Semicolons:
     Python:  No semicolons at end of statements
     Apex:    Semicolons required at end of every statement

  5. Curly braces:
     Python:  Indentation defines blocks
     Apex:    Curly braces {} define blocks

  6. Swap:
     Python:  a, b = b, a         (elegant tuple unpacking)
     Apex:    Must use temp variable:
              Integer temp = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[minIdx]; arr[minIdx] = temp;

  7. Array methods:
     Python:  len(arr)
     Apex:    arr.size()

  8. Access modifiers:
     Python:  No access modifiers (everything is public by default)
     Apex:    public, private, protected, global


ACCESS MODIFIERS IN APEX

  private:   Only accessible within the same class
  public:    Accessible from other classes in the same namespace/package
  protected: Accessible within class and subclasses
  global:    Accessible from everywhere, including external orgs and APIs

  For REST API endpoints, must use "global".


