        if (arr == null) return arr;

  Guard clause: If input is null (nothing passed), return null immediately.
  This prevents NullPointerException on arr.size() below.
  Apex uses null (not None like Python) for absent values.


        Integer n = arr.size();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {

  arr.size(): Returns number of elements (equivalent to Python's len(arr))
  for loop: Classic C-style for loop.
    Initialize: Integer i = 0  (start at 0)
    Condition:  i < n - 1      (continue while true)
    Update:     i++            (increment i by 1 each iteration)

  Why n-1 and not n?
    After the last iteration (i = n-2), the last element (index n-1) is
    automatically in the correct position.
    So we don't need to process the very last element separately.


            Integer minIdx = i;
            for (Integer j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) {
                    minIdx = j;
                }
            }

  Same selection sort logic as Python version.
  Assume minimum is at i.
  Search through unsorted portion (i+1 to n-1).
  Update minIdx when smaller element found.


            if (minIdx != i) {
                Integer temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[minIdx];
                arr[minIdx] = temp;
            }

  Only swap if we actually found a smaller element.
  (Optimization: skips unnecessary same-position swaps)

  Three-line swap (no tuple unpacking in Apex):
    Save arr[i] in temp variable
    Overwrite arr[i] with the minimum (arr[minIdx])
    Overwrite arr[minIdx] with the saved value (temp)


