KEY CONCEPTS GLOSSARY

Graph:
  Collection of vertices (nodes) connected by edges.

Adjacency List:
  Dictionary representation: {vertex: [list of neighbors]}

Heuristic:
  An educated guess/estimate used to guide search toward goal.

Manhattan Distance:
  Distance measure: |row1-row2| + |col1-col2|. No diagonal movement.

Admissible Heuristic:
  Never overestimates the true cost. Guarantees A* finds optimal solution.

Union-Find:
  Data structure tracking which vertices are in the same component.
  Used to detect cycles in Kruskal's algorithm.

Path Compression:
  Optimization in Union-Find: make nodes point directly to root.

Backtracking:
  Algorithm strategy: try an option, if invalid undo it, try next option.

Greedy Algorithm:
  Always makes the locally best choice, hoping for global optimum.

MST (Minimum Spanning Tree):
  Spanning tree connecting all vertices with minimum total edge weight.

Spanning Tree:
  V vertices connected with V-1 edges and no cycles.

Relaxation:
  Update shortest distance if a better (shorter) path is found.

Priority Queue:
  Data structure that always retrieves the minimum (or maximum) element first.

Regular Expression (Regex):
  Pattern for matching text. | means OR, . means any character, etc.

